<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/intermediate/world-epics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Epics are <strong>cultures' founding poems</strong>: long narratives of heroes, gods and
    origins that encode what a people thinks a human being is for. Read individually they
    are masterpieces; read comparatively — Gilgamesh against Homer, the Mahabharata against
    Sundiata — they reveal something neither shows alone: a shared deep pattern (the journey
    outward, the trial, the return changed) wearing utterly different costumes, because each
    culture disagrees about what the journey is for.
  </p>
  <p>
    This atlas lesson also quietly runs the whole discipline past you: oral-formulaic
    composition (how such enormous poems could exist before writing), translation and
    textual criticism (how we get the words at all), and the literary-theory ladder from
    formalism to postcolonialism (how each school reads the same poem differently). By the
    end, "the epic" will look less like a genre and more like a technology for making a
    culture legible to itself.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four founding poems, one shared pattern, different purposes" widget="equationStep" config={epicCfg} />

  <h2>Gilgamesh: mortality as the first plot</h2>
  <p>
    Start at the beginning of world literature. Sumerian poems about King Gilgamesh of
    Uruk circulated from around 2100 BCE; the standard Babylonian version we read was fixed
    around 1200 BCE on twelve tablets. The plot is startlingly modern: a tyrant king; the
    wild man Enkidu, made by the gods to balance him; their friendship; Enkidu's death; and
    Gilgamesh's furious, grief-stricken quest for immortality — he crosses the waters of
    death to find Utnapishtim, survivor of the great flood, who offers him a test (stay
    awake for a week) that he fails in hours, asleep. The plant of youth is retrieved, then
    eaten by a serpent while Gilgamesh bathes. He returns empty-handed and writes the walls:
    the poem's last lines ask you to look at the city wall he built. Immortality is
    transmitted by civilisation and story, not by the body — the thesis of literature
    itself, stated in its first surviving masterpiece.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tablet XI contains Utnapishtim's flood narrative — a man warned by a god, a boat,
    animals, birds sent out to find land — a story pattern so close to Genesis that its
    1872 translation by George Smith caused a sensation (he famously jumped up and paced
    the room, "I am the first man to read that after two thousand years"). The precedent is
    roughly a millennium older than the earliest biblical texts, and it makes Gilgamesh a
    document of shared ancient Near Eastern storytelling as much as a Babylonian poem.
  </p>

  <h2>The Iliad: rage, honour, and dual vision</h2>
  <p>
    Homer's Iliad (composed by the eighth century BCE) announces itself in one word: the
    poem's first line is "Sing, goddess, the anger (mēnin) of Peleus' son Achilles". Not
    the Trojan War — the poem covers about three weeks in the war's tenth year and ends
    before Troy falls. The anger is structural: Achilles, insulted by Agamemnon, withdraws;
    the Greeks bleed; his beloved Patroclus dies wearing his armour; rage returns and burns
    through the poem until, in the final book, the aged king Priam comes to ransom his son
    Hector's body, and the two enemies weep together — Priam for Hector, Achilles for his
    father. The poem's greatest scene is not a battle but that embrace, which is why the
    Iliad has never stopped being read as an anti-war poem written entirely from inside
    war.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its other signature is <strong>dual vision</strong>: Trojans are as fully human as
    Greeks. Hector's farewell to Andromache, the baby frightened by his helmet's plume, is
    an enemy soldier given the poem's tenderest domestic scene. Comparative point: Gilgamesh
    mourns one friend; Homer mourns both armies. That widening of the frame — who counts as
    a person in the poem — is one of the measurable things epics do across cultures.
  </p>

  <h2>The Mahabharata: duty on a battlefield</h2>
  <p>
    India's Mahabharata is the largest poem humanity has produced: roughly 100,000
    verses — about seven times the Iliad and Odyssey combined — traditionally attributed to
    the sage Vyasa, compiled across centuries. Its spine is a dynastic war between cousins
    (Pandavas against Kauravas) for a kingdom; its texture is everything a civilisation
    wanted to preserve — law, cosmology, folk tales, philosophy — layered around that war.
    The innermost layer is the Bhagavad Gita (about 700 verses inside Book 6): the warrior
    Arjuna, seeing his teachers and cousins arrayed against him, refuses to fight, and his
    charioteer Krishna argues him toward action — a full-dress debate about dharma (duty,
    right order) conducted between the two armies. Ethics staged mid-battlefield: no other
    epic puts its philosophy inside its war scene.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the frame is self-aware in a way that anticipates the novel lesson's reflexivity:
    the poem presents itself as told by the bard Ugrasravas to sages in a forest, who heard
    it from a seer, who heard it from Vyasa — a nested chain of tellings in which the
    listener keeps interrupting with questions. The epic's own famous claim about itself:
    what is found here may be found elsewhere; what is not here is nowhere. Comparative
    point: Gilgamesh's king learns wisdom by failing; Arjuna is taught it by argument. Each
    epic's hero is calibrated to what its culture thinks virtue requires — and the
    Mahabharata, unlike its siblings, is deeply suspicious that any war can be clean, ending
    in a heaven that feels uncanny and grief-tinged.
  </p>

  <h2>Sundiata and the living oral epics</h2>
  <p>
    Africa's great case is the Sundiata epic, the founding narrative of the Mali Empire
    (Sundiata Keita defeated the Susu at Kirina around 1235 and built the empire that made
    Timbuktu wealthy). What makes it methodologically precious is that it is still
    performed: the griots — hereditary bard-historians of Mande society — recite it today,
    and the best-known written version (D. T. Niane, 1960) was transcribed from the griot
    Mamadou Kouyaté, who opens by declaring his lineage's authority to tell it. The story
    runs: a crippled child mocked at court, exile, the lion king's rise, magic, an empire
    founded on a charter dividing power among clans. Compare the pattern with the sibling
    epics and note what Sundiata adds: the hero's destiny is communal state-building, not
    personal glory — the epic as constitution.
  </p>
  <p>
    Around these four stand the rest of the world's epic shelf: Beowulf (Anglo-Saxon, in a
    single manuscript copied around the year 1000), the Maya Popol Vuh (K'iche' creation
    and hero narratives, transcribed secretly in the 1550s under colonial rule), China's
    Journey to the West (the monkey pilgrim, published 1592). Each is a culture telling
    itself its own origin and values — and each survives by a different channel (manuscript,
    transcription, performance), which is the next layer of the lesson.
  </p>

  <h2>How epics were made: the oral-formulaic engine</h2>
  <p>
    How could a poem the size of the Iliad exist before writing was common? The answer won
    Milman Parry and Albert Lord their place in scholarship: <strong>composition in
    performance from formulas</strong>. Bards did not memorise a fixed text; they assembled
    lines live from ready-made phrase blocks that fit the meter ("swift-footed Achilles",
    "rosy-fingered Dawn", the arming sequence, the feast sequence), and larger theme-blocks
    (assembly, duel, lament) that expand or contract as performance time demands. Lord's
    fieldwork recording Yugoslav bards in the 1930s proved living singers doing exactly
    this — producing a thousand-line poem in an evening — and the Homeric poems are saturated
    with the same formula system. Consequence for analysis: Homer's repetitions are not
    laziness but the engine; and the "text" we read is one performance-shaped tradition
    that writing later froze. The Mahabharata shows the same signature at a larger scale:
    layers of accumulation over centuries, not a single authorial plan.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> oral does not mean sloppy or primitive. Formulaic
  composition produces extraordinary density — every epithet carries metrical and thematic
  information, and the tradition's stability across centuries (measured by comparing
  independent manuscript branches) is an engineering feat of memory technology.</p>

  <h2>Translation, textual criticism, and the theory ladder</h2>
  <p>
    Two crafts stand between you and every epic. <strong>Textual criticism</strong>
    reconstructs the best possible text from competing manuscripts: there is no "original"
    Homer (Alexandrian editors began standardising him in the third century BCE), and for
    the Mahabharata the Pune Critical Edition (1919–1966) compared hundreds of manuscripts
    to print the shortest common text with all variants recorded. Gilgamesh is physically
    fragmentary — clay tablets broken, key passages missing — and new fragments still make
    headlines; scholars restore gaps with square brackets you can see in the translations.
    <strong>Translation</strong> then decides what the text means: Emily Wilson's Odyssey
    (2017) — the first published English translation by a woman — rendered Polyphemus's
    "my name is Nobody" trick and the poem's violence in iambic pentameter, forcing
    thousands of small interpretive verdicts. Read two translations of one passage and you
    are doing comparative literature in miniature.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, the theory ladder, because each school reads epics differently. Formalism and
    New Criticism read the device: the formulas, the ring composition, the similes. New
    Criticism read each poem as a self-contained machine. Structuralism and the myth
    critics (Propp's tale-functions, Campbell's "hero's journey", 1949) hunted the shared
    pattern — departure, trials, return — and found it everywhere, sometimes too
    everywhere. Postcolonial criticism reads the politics: Sundiata's state-building
    charter, the Popol Vuh's survival under Spanish suppression, Caliban's curse in the
    Shakespeare lesson — who gets to narrate the founding? And the moderns responded with
    rewrites: Joyce's Ulysses (1922) mapped one Dublin day onto Homer; Derek Walcott's
    Omeros (1990) gave the epic to a Caribbean fishing village and won him the 1992 Nobel
    Prize. Epics stay alive precisely because each era re-translates and re-stages them —
    a new Odyssey translation appears every few decades, the Mahabharata runs as television,
    and Gilgamesh keeps surfacing tablet by tablet.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    A quantitative lens sharpened the field: scholars count formula density per hundred
    lines to argue composition method, and trace the ethical sequence across the shelf —
    hero as strength (Gilgamesh), as honour (Achilles), as duty (Arjuna), as conscience
    (Aeneas, carrying his household gods from burning Troy) — reading epics in order reads
    the history of ethics, with each culture's nightmare visible in what its hero fears
    most: death for Gilgamesh, disgrace for Achilles, cosmic disorder for Arjuna, failure
    of duty for Aeneas.
  </p>
  <p>
    Open questions remain genuinely open. Was "Homer" one poet, two (one for each poem), or
    a tradition that finally crystallised in one place — the "Homeric question" remains
    live, now fought with computational stylometry rather than argument alone. Did the
    Homeric poets know Gilgamesh (eastern echoes in the Odyssey's flood-adjacent tales are
    debated)? And textual criticism has an epic of its own still running: the Mahabharata's
    Pune edition compressed a mountain of variants, but regional performance traditions
    — including living oral versions in India — preserve material the manuscript tradition
    lost. The epics, in other words, are not finished books. They are the longest-running
    collaborative texts our species keeps, and the scholarly edition is just their latest
    performance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two structural curiosities belong to the expert shelf. <strong>Ring
    composition</strong> — narrative that returns to its starting image in reverse order,
    a common oral-formulaic device — organises whole books of the Iliad and the Odyssey's
    opening books, and once you see it you notice how much epic structure is circular
    rather than linear. And survival is chancy in a measurable way: Beowulf exists in one
    manuscript (the Nowell Codex, copied around the year 1000, scorched in a 1731 fire
    and patched with eighteenth-century guesses at lost letters); every other Anglo-Saxon
    heroic epic is lost. The canon of world epics is, in part, an accident of copyists.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> George, The Epic of Gilgamesh (trans.); Lord, The Singer of Tales (1960); van Buitenen, The Mahabharata (trans.); Niane, Sundiata: An Epic of Old Mali (1960); Wilson, The Odyssey (2017); Walcott, Omeros (1990).</p>
